\cf3\ul\b\f0 N\ulnone ew\cf0\b0  clears all the segments loaded in memory.\par
\par
\cf3\ul\b O\ulnone pen binary file...\cf0\b0  loads an Atari binary file from your hard disk. This file must have the binary header ($FF $FF) to be recognized.\par
\par
\cf3\b Open \ul d\ulnone isk image file...\cf0\b0  loads an Atari binary file from .ATR/.XFD disk images. \b Only DOS 2.5 disks are supported.\b0  The disk can be a single or enhanced density disk.\par
\par
\cf3\b Open disk image \ul b\ulnone oot...\cf0\b0  loads boot sectors from .ATR/.XFD disk images. Boot sectors are considered to be 128 bytes long.\par
\par
\cf3\b Open disk image \ul s\ulnone ectors...\cf0\b0  loads any part of any sectors from .ATR/.XFD disk images. All densities/sizes are allowed: single, enhanced, double, quad or even larger (hard disk)\par
\par
\cf3\b Save disassembly \ul l\ulnone isting...\cf0\b0  saves on your hard disk the listing generated by DI6502.\par
\par
\cf3\b Load \ul w\ulnone orkspace...\cf0\b0  loads your work where you left it when you saved it with "Save workspace...". Without this option, each time you run the program, you start disassembling from scratch. Now you can set byte type, save workspace, and come back later in DIS6502 to continue working on the disassembly.\par
\par
\cf3\b Save wor\ul k\ulnone space...\cf0\b0  saves your work on your hard disk so you resume it later. \b This option does not save Assembler format.\b0  You have to do it yourself with another menu.\par
\par
\cf3\b E\ul x\ulnone it\cf0\b0  quits DIS6502. \b BE CAREFUL: It doesn't ask you to save your work if you didn't do it.\b0\par
\f2\par
