\f0 In our RAMDISK.COM example, we see that address $3B5D is the start address of the program because this address appears in the second segment at address $02E0.\par
\par
Select "Edit..." option from "Labels" menu. A dialog box appears. At this time, there is no labels defined. Enter 3B5D in Addr field and START in Label field and click on Add/Modify button. Then select OK.\par
\par
Now, each time the address 3B5D appears in the listing, DIS6502 replaces it by its corresponding label START.\par
\par
You can define a label with an offset. For example, if you see that address 3B5E is referenced in the disassembly listing, you could either define another label like BEGIN or use START with an offset. In the previous dialog box, enter 3B5E in Addr field and START+1 in label field.\par
\par
This will tell DIS6502 to replace all addresses 3B5E by START+1 but DIS6502 won't define this label because it can not write START+1 = $3B5E in the disassembly listing. Only START should be defined. \b DIS6502 expects that you define START just as we did previously.\b0\par
