\f0 The disassembler is compatible with MAC/65 and it's highly customizable. In the \b Options\b0  menu, choose \b Assembler format\b0  option. It will open a dialog box where you can customize the disassembler to meet your assembler syntax.\par
\par
When you have entered all the parameters for your assembler, you can save them into a \b .PRF file\b0 . This chapter explains all the fields of the dialog box.\par
You can load the .PRF file corresponding to your assembler by clicking on the \b Load profile\b0  button.\par
\par
Three .PRF files are given:\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\b MAC/65\b0 , \par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Assembler/Editor cartridge\b0 , \par
\b{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}LADS\b0 .\par
\pard\par
LADS is an assembler that does not support many directives. It does not support .WORD and .SBYTE, does not want labels or equates in .BYTE, uses space as value separator in .BYTE,...In fact, it was very good to see if the disassembler could be customized !\par
\par
\b If you want DIS6502 to load your profile automatically, save your settings in a file called ATARI.PRF, ORIC.PRF or 5200.PRF depending on computer mode. On startup, DIS6502 tries to load this file.\b0\par
\par
\i NOTE\i0 : If you use any other assembler and make a .PRF for it, please send it to my email address shown in the About box of the disassembler.\par
\par
When you open the 'Assembler format' dialog box, you see 3 sections called:\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\cf2\ul General\cf0\ulnone , \par
\cf2\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Directive syntax\cf0\ulnone , \par
\cf2\ul{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}Include files\cf0\ulnone .\par
\pard\par
The fields of these 3 sections are explained below:\par
\par
\cf2\b\fs28 General section\par
\cf0\fs24\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\cf3 Comment\cf0\b0  \par
\pard\li720\par
The only comments added to the listing are full line comments (no comments after an instruction for exemple). You can define here the string for a comment.\par
\par
Default: ;\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\cf3\b Hex prefix\cf0\b0  \par
\pard\li720\par
String identifying an hexadecimal value.\par
\par
Default: $\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\cf3\b Illegal instructions\cf0\b0  \par
\pard\li720\par
If your assembler supports all illegal instructions, you can check this option. The disassembler will disassemble all the illegal instructions and will write them in lowercase so you can recognize them in the listing.\par
\par
Default: OFF\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\cf3\b Line numbering\cf0\b0  \par
\pard\li720\par
Used to know if the disassembler should generate line numbers.\par
\par
Default: OFF\par
\par
\i Note\i0 : MAC/65 uses line numbering but I set the default to OFF because you can use the ENTER #D:filename.ASM,A option to load the source and add line numbers at loading time.\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f7\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf7\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\cf3\b Align instructions\cf0\b0  \par
\pard\li720\par
This option is used to align instructions.\par
\par
